<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mykola Skrypnyk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mykola Skrypnyk was a Ukrainian Bolshevik who served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-secretariat">People's Secretariat</a> of the Soviet Ukrainian government formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> in December 1917. Later, as People's Commissar of Education of Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, he promoted Ukrainian language and culture under the policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainisation">Ukrainisation</a>. Attacked as a nationalist when Stalin reversed that policy, he took his own life in 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mykola-skrypnyk.jpg" alt="Mykola Skrypnyk" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown-anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skrypnyk_Mykola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Skrypnyk was born in the village of Yasynuvata in the Yekaterinoslav Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, the son of a railway <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> operator and a midwife. At school he came to know the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taras-shevchenko">Taras Shevchenko</a> and Panteleimon Kulish. He joined the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party in 1898, and after his arrest in 1901 while studying at the Saint Petersburg State Institute of Technology he became a full-time revolutionary.</p><p>Over the next years he was arrested between fifteen and seventeen times, exiled seven times and at one point sentenced to death. He escaped from exile more than once, once covering 1,200 versts by boat and on foot, and worked as a party organiser in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Tsaritsyn</a>, Saratov, Odesa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, Riga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. In 1914 he sat on the editorial board of Pravda.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Amnestied after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a>, he sat on the Military Revolutionary Committee of the Petrograd Soviet during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a>. In March 1918 he replaced Yevhenia Bosch as head of the Soviet Ukrainian government, which German troops soon drove out. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> faction of the Ukrainian Bolsheviks, who wanted a separate Ukrainian party; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> congress overturned that in July 1918. He then worked for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> and, back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, led the suppression of the rebellion of Danylo Terpylo.</p><p>In the debates of 1922 before the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> he opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>'s plan to absorb the republics into the Russian SFSR, calling it thinly disguised Russian chauvinism. After this defeat he turned to culture. As head of education from 1927 he pressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainisation">Ukrainization</a> policy despite a lack of teachers and textbooks, and he convened the Kharkiv Orthographic Conference of 1927, whose spelling, the Skrypnykivka, was adopted in 1928. He nonetheless opposed Ukrainian nationalism and testified against Mykola Khvylovy and Oleksandr Shumsky.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In January 1933 Stalin sent Pavel Postyshev to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> to reverse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainisation">Ukrainization</a> and purge the party. Skrypnyk was removed from his post and his followers were condemned as counter-revolutionary nationalists. Rather than recant in a show trial, he shot himself at his desk in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> apartment on 7 July 1933. His second wife, Raisa Khavina, was executed in 1938. He was rehabilitated in 1962.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mykola Skrypnyk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Stalinist terror struck National Communists, specialists and the intelligentsia; <b>Mykola Skrypnyk</b> and Mykola Khvylyovy were driven to suicide, and hundreds of thousands were executed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The government, called the People's Secretariat, included Yevhenia Bosh, <b>Mykola Skrypnyk</b> and Volodymyr Zatonsky.</blockquote><a href="/state/ukrainian-peoples-republic-of-soviets">Ukrainian People's Republic of Soviets</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yasynuvata, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.12,37.83&amp;year=1911" aria-label="Yasynuvata on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5430 480 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mykola Skrypnyk"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5880 750h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5880 750h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.12,37.83&amp;year=1911">What was here in 1911? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
